.\" Written by hand. Check it with: groff -man -Tutf8 -ww -z docs/man/packaging.toml.5
.TH PACKAGING.TOML 5 "" "PackagingOnLinux" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
packaging.toml \- description of an application to package with PackagingOnLinux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I packaging.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
A TOML file that describes one application: its metadata, the prebuilt files to package
for each platform, and the formats to build.
It is read by
.BR packagingonlinux (1)
(\fBcheck\fR, \fBbuild\fR, \fBgui\fR) and written by \fBpackagingonlinux init\fR and by the
graphical interface, so a project set up in the GUI builds the same packages in CI.
.PP
Relative paths are resolved against the directory that contains the file, not the current
directory.
A list of strings can also be written as one comma\-separated string.
Unknown sections are an error.
.PP
The graphical interface rewrites the whole file when it saves: comments are lost, and keys
equal to their default are left out.
.SH "[app] SECTION"
The application metadata, shared by every format.
.TP
\fBid\fR (string, required)
Reverse\-DNS application id, at least three dot\-separated parts, e.g.
\fBio.github.you.MyApp\fR.
It names the .desktop file, the icon and the AppStream metainfo, and is the Flatpak app id.
.TP
\fBname\fR (string, required)
Display name, e.g. \fBMyApp\fR.
The package name used by deb and rpm is derived from it: lowercase, with runs of
characters other than a\-z, 0\-9, +, . and \- replaced by \-.
.TP
\fBversion\fR (string, required)
Starts with a digit and contains only letters, digits, ., + and ~ (no \- and no spaces),
e.g. \fB1.2.0\fR or \fB1.2.0~rc1\fR.
.TP
\fBsummary\fR (string, required)
One\-line description: the .desktop Comment, the package summary, the AppStream summary.
.TP
\fBdescription\fR (string)
Long description; blank lines separate paragraphs.
Default: the summary.
.TP
\fBlicense\fR (string)
SPDX license expression, e.g. \fBMIT\fR or \fBGPL\-3.0\-only\fR.
Default: \fBLicenseRef\-proprietary\fR.
.TP
\fBmaintainer\fR (string)
\fBName <email>\fR.
.TP
\fBhomepage\fR (string)
Project URL.
.TP
\fBcategories\fR (list of strings)
freedesktop menu categories, e.g. \fB["Development", "IDE"]\fR.
The main ones are AudioVideo, Audio, Video, Development, Education, Game, Graphics,
Network, Office, Science, Settings, System and Utility.
The first one also picks the deb Section.
Default: \fB["Utility"]\fR.
.SH "[linux] SECTION"
What to package for the Linux formats.
Needed to build them; leave it out to build only Windows formats.
Set exactly one of \fBbinary\fR or \fBdir\fR.
.TP
\fBbinary\fR (path)
A single executable, installed as \fI/usr/bin/<name>\fR.
.TP
\fBdir\fR (path)
An install folder, copied to \fI/usr/lib/<package>/\fR, with \fI/usr/bin/<exec name>\fR a
symlink to its executable.
.TP
\fBexec\fR (string, required with \fBdir\fR)
Path of the executable inside \fBdir\fR, e.g. \fBbin/myapp\fR.
.TP
\fBicon\fR (path)
A PNG or SVG file.
A square PNG of 16, 22, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128, 256 or 512 pixels, or an SVG, goes to the
hicolor icon theme; any other PNG goes to \fI/usr/share/pixmaps\fR with a warning, which
Flatpak does not accept.
Without an icon, menus show a generic one.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR (string)
Shell command run before packaging, see \fBBUILD COMMANDS\fR.
.PP
The architecture is read from the executable's ELF header; a script is packaged as
architecture\-independent (deb \fBall\fR, rpm \fBnoarch\fR).
.SH "[windows] SECTION"
What to package for the Windows formats (\fBzip\fR, \fBnsis\fR): the same keys as
\fB[linux]\fR, with a Windows executable (\fI.exe\fR).
The architecture (x86, x64, arm64) is read from its PE header.
An empty \fB[windows]\fR section still means "build for Windows".
.TP
\fBicon\fR (path)
An \fI.ico\fR file, or a square PNG of at most 256 pixels.
Default: the \fB[linux]\fR icon, when it can be converted.
.SH "[deps] SECTION"
Dependencies of the Linux packages (optional).
.TP
\fBauto\fR (boolean)
Detect the shared libraries the executable needs: \fBdpkg\-shlibdeps\fR (or ldd and
\fBdpkg \-S\fR) for deb, rpmbuild's automatic requirements for rpm.
Set it to \fBfalse\fR for a script or an app that bundles its libraries.
Default: \fBtrue\fR.
.TP
\fBdeb\fR (list of strings)
Extra deb dependencies, in Debian syntax, e.g. \fB["python3 (>= 3.11)", "libgtk\-3\-0"]\fR.
.TP
\fBrpm\fR (list of strings)
Extra rpm requirements, e.g. \fB["python3 >= 3.11", "gtk3"]\fR.
.SH "[flatpak] SECTION"
Options of the \fBflatpak\fR format (optional).
An unknown key is an error.
.TP
\fBruntime\fR (string)
The runtime the app runs on, installed from Flathub on first use.
Default: \fBorg.freedesktop.Platform\fR.
.TP
\fBruntime_version\fR (string)
Default: \fB26.08\fR.
.TP
\fBsdk\fR (string)
Default: the runtime's SDK (\fBorg.freedesktop.Platform\fR \-> \fBorg.freedesktop.Sdk\fR).
.TP
\fBfinish_args\fR (list of strings)
The sandbox permissions, as
.BR flatpak\-build\-finish (1)
options.
Default: \fB["\-\-share=ipc", "\-\-socket=wayland", "\-\-socket=fallback\-x11",
"\-\-device=dri"]\fR: the display, the GPU and IPC, but not the user's files (add e.g.
\fB"\-\-filesystem=home"\fR) nor the network (\fB"\-\-share=network"\fR).
.SH "[snap] SECTION"
Options of the \fBsnap\fR format (optional).
An unknown key is an error.
.TP
\fBbase\fR (string)
The base snap the app runs on.
Building on the host (\fBSNAPCRAFT_BUILD_ENVIRONMENT=host\fR) needs the matching Ubuntu
release (24.04 for \fBcore24\fR).
Default: \fBcore24\fR.
.TP
\fBconfinement\fR (string)
\fBstrict\fR (only the \fBplugs\fR below), \fBclassic\fR (the same access as a deb; the
Snap Store accepts it only after a manual review) or \fBdevmode\fR.
Default: \fBstrict\fR.
.TP
\fBgrade\fR (string)
\fBstable\fR or \fBdevel\fR.
Default: empty, meaning \fBdevel\fR when the version contains a \fB~\fR (a pre\-release),
\fBstable\fR otherwise.
.TP
\fBplugs\fR (list of strings)
The interfaces a strict snap connects to (ignored for a classic snap).
Default: \fB["desktop", "desktop\-legacy", "wayland", "x11", "opengl", "home",
"network"]\fR.
.TP
\fBextensions\fR (list of strings)
snapcraft extensions, e.g. \fB["gnome"]\fR for a GTK app or \fB["kde\-neon\-6"]\fR for
a Qt 6 app.
Default: none.
.TP
\fBstage_packages\fR (list of strings)
Ubuntu packages to bundle in the snap, for libraries the base does not have.
Default: none.
.SH "[output] SECTION"
.TP
\fBformats\fR (list of strings)
Formats built when \fBpackagingonlinux build\fR has no \fB\-f\fR option.
The list of formats, with the tools each one needs, is in
.BR packagingonlinux (1)
and \fBpackagingonlinux \-\-help\fR.
Default: \fB["deb", "rpm"]\fR.
.TP
\fBdir\fR (path)
Where the packages are written (overridden by \fB\-o\fR).
Default: \fBdist\fR.
.SH "BUILD COMMANDS"
The \fBcommand\fR of \fB[linux]\fR or \fB[windows]\fR runs with
.BR sh (1)
in the config's directory before that platform is packaged, and only when a format of that
platform is built: \fB\-f deb\fR never runs the Windows command.
It typically compiles the app or assembles its \fBdir\fR.
If it fails, that platform's formats fail and the others are still built.
It receives these environment variables, on top of the caller's:
.TP
.B APP_ID
The \fB[app]\fR id.
.TP
.B APP_NAME
The \fB[app]\fR name.
.TP
.B APP_VERSION
The \fB[app]\fR version, e.g. to stamp it into the binary.
.TP
.B APP_PLATFORM
\fBlinux\fR or \fBwindows\fR.
.SH "INSTALLED FILES"
Every Linux format installs the same tree, built from freedesktop standards only, so the
app shows in the menus of any desktop environment:
.PP
.nf
.RS
/usr/bin/<exec name>
/usr/lib/<package>/                         (with \fBdir\fR)
/usr/share/applications/<id>.desktop
/usr/share/icons/hicolor/<size>/apps/<id>.png|.svg
/usr/share/metainfo/<id>.metainfo.xml
.RE
.fi
.SH "OLD LAYOUT"
Configs written before the \fB[linux]\fR section existed, with \fB[files]\fR (binary, dir,
exec, icon) and \fB[build]\fR (command), are still read as \fB[linux]\fR.
Using both layouts in one file is an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
A single Linux binary built by \fBmake\fR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[app]
id = "io.github.you.MyApp"
name = "MyApp"
version = "1.0.0"
summary = "Does one thing well"
license = "MIT"
maintainer = "You <you@example.org>"
categories = ["Utility"]

[linux]
command = "make"
binary = "build/myapp"
icon = "icons/myapp.svg"

[output]
formats = ["deb", "rpm", "tarball", "appimage"]
.RE
.fi
.PP
An install folder for Linux and a Windows build cross\-compiled with Go:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[app]
id = "io.github.you.MyApp"
name = "MyApp"
version = "2.1.0"
summary = "Does one thing well"

[linux]
command = "make install DESTDIR=build/linux"
dir = "build/linux/opt/myapp"
exec = "bin/myapp"
icon = "icons/myapp.png"

[windows]
command = "GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build \-o build/myapp.exe"
binary = "build/myapp.exe"

[deps]
deb = ["libgtk\-3\-0"]
rpm = ["gtk3"]

[output]
formats = ["deb", "rpm", "zip", "nsis"]
dir = "dist"
.RE
.fi
